<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  hexapod.ros2_control.xacro : the hardware abstraction layer
  ==========================================================================
  WHAT ros2_control IS, AND WHY IT EXISTS

  Without it you would write a Gazebo plugin that subscribes to some topic
  and sets joint forces. Then, for real hardware, you would write a
  completely different node that talks to servos. The two would diverge, and
  the gait you tuned in simulation would not run on the robot. That is the
  problem ros2_control solves.

  It defines ONE interface between "something that can move joints" and
  "something that decides where joints should go":

      CONTROLLERS  (trajectory, position, gait)
            |  command interfaces  /  state interfaces
            v
      RESOURCE MANAGER
            |
      HARDWARE INTERFACE   (this is the swappable part)
            |
      +=====+======+
      |            |
    Gazebo      Real servos

  Phase 9 replaces only the bottom box. The controllers, the URDF, the gait
  code and the launch files are untouched. That is the entire payoff.

  THE THREE PIECES OF VOCABULARY YOU NEED

  command_interface : something a controller can WRITE. We expose "position"
                      because servos are commanded by angle.
  state_interface   : something a controller can READ. We expose position,
                      velocity and effort.
  controller_manager: the real time loop. Every cycle it reads all state
                      interfaces, runs each active controller's update(), and
                      writes all command interfaces. Nothing else may touch
                      the hardware.

  WHY POSITION AND NOT EFFORT
  A hobby or serial bus servo has an internal position loop; you send it an
  angle, not a torque. Modelling it as an effort source would mean tuning 18
  PID loops in simulation whose gains have no counterpart on the real robot.
  Position command is the honest abstraction here.

  Note the consequence, stated plainly so it does not surprise you later:
  gazebo_ros2_control's GazeboSystem drives a position command interface
  KINEMATICALLY (it sets the joint angle directly rather than solving for the
  torque that would achieve it). Joints will therefore not visibly sag under
  load, and the <limit effort> value is not strictly enforced. That is
  realistic for a good digital servo and unrealistic for a weak one. It is
  also why the static torque analysis in common_properties.xacro matters:
  the simulation will NOT warn you that your servos are too small.
==========================================================================  -->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- ====================================================================
       CONTROL MODE: position or effort
       ====================================================================

       THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT DECISION IN THE SIMULATION, and we now
       have experimental evidence for it rather than opinion.

       Two tests settled it:

         fix_base:=true        robot anchored, gait running, feet loaded
                               -> completely stable
         controllers:=false    no controller writes to any joint
                               -> robot collapses and then stays put

       So contact, friction, inertia and the solver are all sound. The only
       configuration that drifts is: floating base AND position commands.

       WHY POSITION COMMANDS BREAK A FLOATING BASE
       gazebo_ros2_control implements a position command with Gazebo's
       Joint::SetPosition(), which TELEPORTS the joint. It does not compute
       the torque required; it overrides the state. For a bolted-down arm
       that is harmless. For a robot standing on six frictional contacts
       with nothing holding its body, each control cycle hands the solver a
       configuration it did not produce, and the inconsistency is resolved
       as a small impulse on the free base. At 200 Hz that integrates into
       slow drift and yaw. No value of kp, kd, mu, timestep or solver
       iterations removes it, because the energy is injected by the
       override, not by the contact model.

       WHY EFFORT COMMANDS FIX IT
       An effort command is a torque. The physics engine integrates it,
       computes the resulting joint motion, and produces reaction forces
       that are consistent with the contacts by construction. Nothing is
       overridden, so no spurious momentum appears. The joint reaches its
       target because a PID loop drives the error to zero, exactly as a real
       servo does.

       AND IT IS MORE HONEST
       A real servo is a torque source wrapped in its own position loop. It
       has finite torque, it droops under load, and it takes time to
       converge. Effort control reproduces all three. Position control
       reproduces none: it hits the target instantly regardless of load, so
       a gait tuned against it can demand things the hardware cannot do and
       you find out on the bench. This is the same argument as the servo
       torque calculation in common_properties.xacro.

       Set via  control_mode:=effort  (default) or  control_mode:=position
       to fall back to the known-working fixed-base demo.
  ==================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="use_effort"
                  value="${'$(arg control_mode)' == 'effort'}"/>

  <xacro:macro name="leg_joint_ros2_control"
               params="joint_name lower upper initial">
    <joint name="${joint_name}">

      <xacro:if value="${use_effort}">
        <!-- Torque command, bounded by the servo's real capability. The
             PID that converts a position target into this torque lives in
             the controller, not here, which is correct: the hardware
             declares what it can accept, the controller decides what to
             send. -->
        <command_interface name="effort">
          <param name="min">${-joint_effort}</param>
          <param name="max">${joint_effort}</param>
        </command_interface>
      </xacro:if>

      <xacro:unless value="${use_effort}">
        <command_interface name="position">
          <param name="min">${lower}</param>
          <param name="max">${upper}</param>
        </command_interface>
      </xacro:unless>

      <!-- initial_value places the joint at its stance angle during hardware
           initialisation, before the first control cycle, so the robot
           exists standing and no large correction is ever needed. Honoured
           identically by a real hardware interface in Phase 9. -->
      <state_interface name="position">
        <param name="initial_value">${initial}</param>
      </state_interface>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
      <state_interface name="effort"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- One macro emits the three joint blocks for a leg, so the 18 joint
       list cannot drift out of sync with leg_macro.xacro. -->
  <xacro:macro name="leg_ros2_control" params="prefix">
    <xacro:leg_joint_ros2_control joint_name="${prefix}_coxa_joint"
                                  lower="${coxa_lower}" upper="${coxa_upper}"
                                  initial="${stance_coxa_angle}"/>
    <xacro:leg_joint_ros2_control joint_name="${prefix}_femur_joint"
                                  lower="${femur_lower}" upper="${femur_upper}"
                                  initial="${stance_femur_angle}"/>
    <xacro:leg_joint_ros2_control joint_name="${prefix}_tibia_joint"
                                  lower="${tibia_lower}" upper="${tibia_upper}"
                                  initial="${stance_tibia_angle}"/>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       THE ros2_control BLOCK

       type="system" means one hardware component owns several joints, as
       opposed to "actuator" (one joint) or "sensor" (read only). All 18
       joints belong to one system because in simulation they share one
       physics engine, and on hardware they will share one serial bus.
  ==================================================================== -->
  <ros2_control name="HexapodSystem" type="system">
    <hardware>
      <!-- Phase 9 swaps THIS LINE and nothing else:
             hexapod_hardware/HexapodServoBus
           The 18 joint blocks below stay byte for byte identical. -->
      <plugin>gazebo_ros2_control/GazeboSystem</plugin>
    </hardware>

    <xacro:leg_ros2_control prefix="lf"/>
    <xacro:leg_ros2_control prefix="lm"/>
    <xacro:leg_ros2_control prefix="lr"/>
    <xacro:leg_ros2_control prefix="rf"/>
    <xacro:leg_ros2_control prefix="rm"/>
    <xacro:leg_ros2_control prefix="rr"/>
  </ros2_control>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       THE GAZEBO PLUGIN

       This is what actually starts a controller_manager INSIDE the Gazebo
       process. That co-location is deliberate: the control loop must run in
       lockstep with the physics step, not asynchronously over DDS, or the
       controller would be reading stale state.

       Because of that, you do NOT run `ros2 run controller_manager
       ros2_control_node` in simulation. Doing so gives you two competing
       controller managers and a very confusing afternoon.

       <parameters> points at the controller YAML. The path is resolved by
       xacro at expansion time, so it must be a real filesystem path in the
       install space, which $(find ...) provides.
  ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- The controller YAML follows sim_profile, because the gains are only
       meaningful against a particular set of masses. repo mode's p=100 is
       tuned against 1e-5 kg links; applying it to the measured model gives
       legs that cannot hold the robot up. Applying our gains to the repo
       model gives an absurdly stiff joint. They are not interchangeable and
       must not be selected independently. -->
  <gazebo>
    <plugin filename="libgazebo_ros2_control.so" name="gazebo_ros2_control">
      <xacro:if value="${repo_mode}">
        <parameters>$(find hexapod_control)/config/controllers_repo.yaml</parameters>
      </xacro:if>
      <xacro:unless value="${repo_mode}">
        <parameters>$(find hexapod_control)/config/controllers.yaml</parameters>
      </xacro:unless>
      <robot_param>robot_description</robot_param>
      <robot_param_node>robot_state_publisher</robot_param_node>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

</robot>
